---
import DeepDiveLayout from '../../layouts/DeepDiveLayout.astro';
---

<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Deep Dive into Adjustability"
  pillar="Adjustability"
  chapterNumber="05"
  nextTitle="Keep Learning"
  nextHref="/learn"
>
  <section>
    <h2>Quick Recap</h2>

    <p>
      Adjustability is the ability to maintain the same swing while allowing
      the baseball to determine when and where contact occurs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Great hitters don't build a different swing for every pitch.
    </p>

    <p>
      They make subtle adjustments while preserving the same principles.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Before We Begin</h2>

    <p>
      Every pillar before this one has been preparing for this moment.
    </p>

    <p>
      The load...
    </p>

    <p>
      The balance...
    </p>

    <p>
      The firing sequence...
    </p>

    <p>
      The barrel path...
    </p>

    <p>
      None of them change.
    </p>

    <p>
      Only the baseball does.
    </p>

    <p>
      Adjustability is the ability to allow the pitch to determine the timing
      and location of contact without abandoning the swing that has already
      been built.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The swing stays the same.<br />
      The contact changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What We Want From Adjustability</h2>

    <p>
      Every hitter should be capable of adjusting to:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Pitch speed.</li>
      <li>Pitch location.</li>
      <li>Pitch height.</li>
      <li>Pitch movement.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      ...without changing the swing itself.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The body adjusts.<br />
      The principles remain.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Speed</h2>

    <p>
      Speed determines <strong>when</strong> the swing begins.
    </p>

    <p>
      It does not change how the swing works.
    </p>

    <p>
      A slower pitch simply gives the hitter more time.
    </p>

    <p>
      A faster pitch demands an earlier decision.
    </p>

    <h3>Location</h3>

    <p>
      Location determines <strong>where</strong> contact occurs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Inside pitches.
    </p>

    <p>
      Middle pitches.
    </p>

    <p>
      Outside pitches.
    </p>

    <p>
      The contact point changes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The swing does not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Trust Your Swing</h2>

    <p>
      One of the biggest mistakes hitters make is believing every pitch
      requires a different swing.
    </p>

    <p>
      It doesn't.
    </p>

    <p>
      A quality swing naturally adjusts because every pillar before this one
      has already prepared the hitter.
    </p>

    <p>
      The more you manipulate the swing during the pitch...
    </p>

    <p>
      The less adjustable you become.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      See it.<br />
      Decide.<br />
      Swing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Drills</h2>

    <div class="drill-grid">

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Soft Toss — Location Progression</h3>
        <p>
          Learn to adjust to middle, inside, outside, high, and low pitches
          while maintaining the same swing.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Colored Ball Recognition</h3>
        <p>
          Train the eyes and brain to process information before committing to
          the swing.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Variable Soft Toss</h3>
        <p>
          Vary the height, rhythm, and timing of each toss to eliminate
          anticipation and promote reaction.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Front Toss Progression</h3>
        <p>
          Apply the same principles against increasing speed while adjusting to
          different pitch locations.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Chaos Toss</h3>
        <p>
          Randomize speed, location, height, timing, and visual recognition to
          combine every pillar into one competitive drill.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Changing the swing for different pitches.</li>
      <li>Rushing because of velocity.</li>
      <li>Lunging at off-speed pitches.</li>
      <li>Reaching for outside pitches.</li>
      <li>Pulling off inside pitches.</li>
      <li>Trying to manipulate the barrel instead of trusting the sequence.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      Great adjustability comes from trusting great fundamentals.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Self Evaluation</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Can I handle different speeds without changing my mechanics?</li>
      <li>Can I adjust to every pitch location?</li>
      <li>Do I trust my swing?</li>
      <li>Am I reacting instead of guessing?</li>
      <li>Can I compete without mechanical thoughts?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</DeepDiveLayout>